WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.449 --> 00:00:01.788
- He's an aerospace engineer,

00:00:01.788 --> 00:00:05.578
noted for designing light,
strong, unusual looking,

00:00:05.578 --> 00:00:07.475
energy efficient aircrafts.

00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:09.810
He designed the record breaking Voyager,

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.330
which was the first plane
to fly around the world

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:14.466
without stopping or refueling.

00:00:14.466 --> 00:00:17.198
He designed the first
privately funded space craft

00:00:17.198 --> 00:00:20.437
to enter space twice
within a two week period.

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:24.604
He's Burt Rutan and here are
his top ten rules for success.

00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:29.835
- [Burt] Someone ask him
what's the most difficult thing

00:00:29.835 --> 00:00:31.467
about going to the moon.

00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.102
- [Man] Copy.

00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:36.533
- And then he says the will to do it.

00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:39.524
The engineering is just calculations

00:00:39.524 --> 00:00:43.490
but the decision and
the will and the courage

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:47.866
to try to do it, is the
most difficult hurdle.

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:49.713
- [Man] (mumbles) 105

00:00:49.713 --> 00:00:52.633
- We are known for trying new things,

00:00:52.633 --> 00:00:55.716
even though we have a tough schedule.

00:00:56.644 --> 00:01:00.811
We did an airplane that the
customer wanted it flying within

00:01:02.916 --> 00:01:06.044
I think it was eight and a half months

00:01:06.044 --> 00:01:09.181
and we elected on our own

00:01:09.181 --> 00:01:12.907
to try a totally new manufacturing method

00:01:12.907 --> 00:01:15.668
for the wings and tail
services and control services.

00:01:15.668 --> 00:01:17.604
Something that hadn't been done before.

00:01:17.604 --> 00:01:19.484
Something that may not work.

00:01:19.484 --> 00:01:21.650
Something that was really
exciting and interesting

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.603
and if it did work,
it's breakthrough stuff.

00:01:24.603 --> 00:01:26.516
Our first astronaut, Alan Shepard,

00:01:26.516 --> 00:01:28.332
who flew a sub-orbinal flight

00:01:28.332 --> 00:01:32.148
followed Gagarin by only three weeks.

00:01:32.148 --> 00:01:36.983
After Shepard's flight, it took
only seven and a half years

00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:41.150
for us to launch Apollo 8 to
go out and orbit the moon.

00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:46.085
They went to the moon on
the very first man flight

00:01:46.085 --> 00:01:47.879
of the big Saturn 5.

00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:50.021
Now can you imagine today,

00:01:50.021 --> 00:01:54.188
NASA developing a brand new,
huge, big advanced rocket

00:01:56.377 --> 00:01:59.462
and the first time people fly on it,

00:01:59.462 --> 00:02:00.670
they go to the moon

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.687
or they go somewhere
we've never been before.

00:02:03.687 --> 00:02:07.044
That puts in perspective
the kind of risks that we,

00:02:07.044 --> 00:02:09.190
that we were taking

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:13.150
and those who put themselves in a position

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.563
to take enormous risks are
those that have breakthroughs

00:02:16.563 --> 00:02:19.958
and those that have research results.

00:02:19.958 --> 00:02:23.071
I believe that confidence in
nonsense is a major factor

00:02:23.071 --> 00:02:26.525
and the reason is, think of
the most important breakthrough

00:02:26.525 --> 00:02:29.042
that you can think of.

00:02:29.042 --> 00:02:31.991
Go back before it was
recognized as a breakthrough

00:02:31.991 --> 00:02:34.077
and the populous normally would consider,

00:02:34.077 --> 00:02:36.492
well that's nonsense.

00:02:36.492 --> 00:02:37.814
That's nonsensical.

00:02:37.814 --> 00:02:39.488
Now all of a sudden when it really is

00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:41.683
recognized as a true
breakthrough then of course,

00:02:41.683 --> 00:02:43.264
oh yeah, that's really cool.

00:02:43.264 --> 00:02:46.607
But what people forget
is it's nonsense before.

00:02:46.607 --> 00:02:47.570
And what that means,

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.332
that's why the weird folk
have the breakthroughs

00:02:50.332 --> 00:02:53.507
because they have confidence in nonsense.

00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:55.315
If it's nonsensical and you say,

00:02:55.315 --> 00:02:56.915
well I'm not going to try to do that,

00:02:56.915 --> 00:02:58.489
it doesn't make sense,

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:00.921
you're not going to have a breakthrough.

00:03:00.921 --> 00:03:04.091
The guy who is silly enough to think that

00:03:04.091 --> 00:03:06.395
Hey I'll try that even
though it's nonsense,

00:03:06.395 --> 00:03:08.579
he's the guy that's going
to have the breakthrough.

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:12.412
So, try things that you
don't think will work.

00:03:14.837 --> 00:03:18.636
If you think they'll work,
you're not doing research anyway.

00:03:18.636 --> 00:03:22.041
You're going to have failures
if you try hard stuff.

00:03:22.041 --> 00:03:25.428
If you go out and try to
build a, a better seaplane

00:03:25.428 --> 00:03:29.102
and your goal is to go 12 knots faster,

00:03:29.102 --> 00:03:31.494
you're probably not going to fail.

00:03:31.494 --> 00:03:34.605
You may fail because it
only goes four knots faster

00:03:34.605 --> 00:03:37.262
but it's not going to
be a disaster failure.

00:03:37.262 --> 00:03:39.239
But if you go out and try something

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:42.822
that is, Holy crap,
can you really do that?

00:03:43.708 --> 00:03:46.208
And it fails, it is a failure.

00:03:47.601 --> 00:03:52.058
And I feel very strongly
that it's not good enough

00:03:52.058 --> 00:03:54.306
for us to have generations of kids

00:03:54.306 --> 00:03:56.842
that think that it's okay to look forward

00:03:56.842 --> 00:03:59.993
to a better version of a
cellphone with a video in it.

00:03:59.993 --> 00:04:02.338
They need to look forward to exploration.

00:04:02.338 --> 00:04:04.457
They need to look forward to colonization.

00:04:04.457 --> 00:04:07.257
They need to look
forward to breakthroughs.

00:04:07.257 --> 00:04:08.340
They need to.

00:04:09.723 --> 00:04:12.505
We need to inspire them
because they need to lead us

00:04:12.505 --> 00:04:15.088
and help us survive in the future.

00:04:15.088 --> 00:04:17.304
What I try to encourage people to do

00:04:17.304 --> 00:04:20.528
is to have a breakthrough by finding a way

00:04:20.528 --> 00:04:22.278
to do it more simply.

00:04:23.769 --> 00:04:27.936
And even if the real simple
one has a chance of not working

00:04:31.549 --> 00:04:35.171
because it's too simple,
well try it anyway.

00:04:35.171 --> 00:04:38.105
Because in trying it,

00:04:38.105 --> 00:04:40.251
sometimes you'll stumble unto a solution

00:04:40.251 --> 00:04:42.256
on why it wouldn't work

00:04:42.256 --> 00:04:44.514
and now you've really had a big gain.

00:04:44.514 --> 00:04:47.115
Now you have a simple thing that does work

00:04:47.115 --> 00:04:49.837
and that's the real challenge now.

00:04:49.837 --> 00:04:53.587
You can always make something
work by adding complexity

00:04:53.587 --> 00:04:56.036
but you can never make
something affordable

00:04:56.036 --> 00:04:58.051
by adding complexity.

00:04:58.051 --> 00:05:02.570
I think breakthroughs occur
not when everybody's doing fine

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:06.737
and when your priority is to
make everyone equally happy,

00:05:08.568 --> 00:05:09.938
you don't have breakthroughs then.

00:05:09.938 --> 00:05:12.905
That's monotony and that
can lead to monotony

00:05:12.905 --> 00:05:16.851
and it can lead, even to
your ability to survive.

00:05:16.851 --> 00:05:21.656
But you have breakthroughs
either during or after crises,

00:05:21.656 --> 00:05:23.943
when you have really bad times.

00:05:23.943 --> 00:05:26.369
We're creative when we're threatened.

00:05:26.369 --> 00:05:27.803
I think the world of aviation

00:05:27.803 --> 00:05:30.886
will gain from me mostly inspiration.

00:05:32.633 --> 00:05:35.227
The fact that a few people

00:05:35.227 --> 00:05:40.149
literally in a garage or a
small shop built an airplane

00:05:40.149 --> 00:05:42.786
that doubled and absolute distance record,

00:05:42.786 --> 00:05:44.411
flew all the way around the world,

00:05:44.411 --> 00:05:47.279
flew through all this weather
and came back home safely,

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:50.390
the fact that that was done all composite

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:51.743
without mixing metals

00:05:51.743 --> 00:05:54.216
without solving all these stupid problems

00:05:54.216 --> 00:05:56.727
that the engineers make for themselves,

00:05:56.727 --> 00:05:59.775
that will provide the inspiration,

00:05:59.775 --> 00:06:01.954
that my God, it can be done.

00:06:01.954 --> 00:06:02.787
We're going to do it.

00:06:02.787 --> 00:06:05.064
We'll stick our necks out.

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:06.759
I knew how the Wright brothers felt

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.647
when they came upon these things

00:06:08.647 --> 00:06:09.980
and had success.

00:06:12.082 --> 00:06:15.398
We decided, this is the
best way to pun this.

00:06:15.398 --> 00:06:17.479
What's one thing

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:21.060
and that was the passion
that I saw in your eyes

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.393
for the outcome.

00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:25.321
And I knew that you would stick with it

00:06:25.321 --> 00:06:28.345
because I knew that passion was genuine.

00:06:28.345 --> 00:06:31.184
When you go out and
look to venture capital

00:06:31.184 --> 00:06:32.416
or look to whatever,

00:06:32.416 --> 00:06:36.113
you're looking for people
who have a passion for money.

00:06:36.113 --> 00:06:39.152
But you have a passion for stage flight

00:06:39.152 --> 00:06:40.934
and I think that's the difference between

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:42.025
- Excuse me.

00:06:42.025 --> 00:06:44.858
(audience laughs)

00:06:49.938 --> 00:06:51.369
